# What this build is supposed to run, per module.
#
# `.github/scripts/test-summary.sh` sums `tests=` out of the JUnit XML. That number describes
# what *ran*; nothing in the build, the workflow or the summary compared it against what should
# have run, and that gap is the one this repo has now found under four different mechanisms —
# a disabled test task, a task that is never wired to `check`, a cached task replayed as
# up-to-date, and a run filtered to an empty set. All four produce the same observation: an
# honest aggregate over a smaller set, with `skipped="0"`, `BUILD SUCCESSFUL`, and no annotation
# anywhere. Enumerating the mechanisms is losing. This file is the other half of the comparison.
#
# Format: one `<module> <minimum tests>` per line; `#` starts a comment. The module name is the
# first path component of `<module>/build/test-results/…`, which is the Gradle project name.
#
# **These are floors, not targets.** Adding tests never needs a change here. Removing or
# consolidating them does, and that is the point: the number is committed, so a drop in coverage
# has to appear in a diff next to the change that caused it, signed by whoever decided it was
# fine. Lowering a floor is a legitimate thing to do — silently falling below one is not.
#
# A module declared `0` is a positive claim that it has no tests. Every module in the build must
# appear: `:root:verifyTestFloors` passes the build's own module roster in
# `AWAKENER_TEST_MODULES` and fails if this file and that roster disagree in either direction,
# so a new module cannot reach `main` without someone stating what it is expected to run.
#
# Counts as of the #43 branch rebased onto `1ca031d`: 32 suites, 347 tests, skipped=0 — per
# module, `cli` 6/60, `config` 5/64, `registry` 10/109, `wm` 11/114, read out of
# `*/build/test-results/jvmTest/*.xml` after a `clean build --no-build-cache` with both REQUIRE
# flags set. The floors below are those counts.
#
# Three of the four were behind before that change, and only one of the three is its doing: it
# adds 4 to `cli` and 4 to `config`, while `registry` had been at 109 against a floor of 102
# since **#127**. The drift note `test-summary.sh` prints named all three on the same line, which
# is the mechanism working — a floor nobody refreshes decays into a number too small to catch
# anything short of a module reporting nothing at all.
#
# **That attribution said #113 until review caught it, and it was wrong in both halves** — which
# is worth the space, because a wrong provenance claim in the file whose whole job is provenance
# is the one kind of error here that propagates: the next person refreshing a floor reads this
# line rather than re-deriving it. So, derived rather than recalled, every figure from
# `git grep -c '@Test' <ref> -- registry/src/jvmTest` summed over the files it prints:
#
#   - The `102` was not stale since #113. **#122 (`2b92504`) set it**, off CI run 31226978882 on
#     `4b2eed0` — a tree that already contained #113 (`610154a`, merged five commits earlier).
#     It was current the day it was written.
#   - `registry` was at **94** at #113, not 109 — and 94 is exactly the floor #119 (`4b2eed0`)
#     created and #122 replaced. `4b2eed0` itself sums to 102 (`StorePermissionsTest`, +8).
#   - The 102 → 109 drift is **#127 (`e19af5f`)**: `ProcessCommandRunnerTest` 7 → 13 `@Test`s and
#     `SpanreedCliTest` 22 → 23. Two commits touch `registry/src/jvmTest` between `4b2eed0` and
#     `main`, and only that one moves the count; `9f6521c` extracted `SpanreedHarness` out of
#     `SpanreedCliTest` and left the sum at 109.
#
# `cli`'s sixtieth is #100's (`9f6521c`), not this branch's: the first run of this change measured
# 59 against `e19af5f`, the rebase onto `9f6521c` brought `AwakeningSwayTest`'s new case with it,
# and the figure above is from a re-run *after* the rebase. Which is the whole reason the ancestry
# check exists — the pre-rebase 59 described a tree that was never going to be merged, and so did
# the 346 this line read before the second rebase, onto `1ca031d` (#129).
#
# The line it replaced named `4b2eed0` (CI run 31226978882, job 93023157970): 31 suites, 331
# tests, `cli` 5/55, `config` 5/60, `registry` 10/102, `wm` 11/114.
#
# The previous line named `3d16851` (321 tests), and was behind `main` before it landed. Nothing
# miscounted, and the ordering is the whole point: those floors came from run 31221639237 on
# `3d16851`, and #119's own CI (run 31226010044, `ce1ba8c`) began at 23:05:27Z — **neither tree
# contained #118**, which merged at 23:09:50Z, 4m23s *after* that run began and 13m36s before
# #119 itself merged. #118's two test classes were therefore never in the counts these floors
# were written from; the counter was right and the tree was simply a different one. So `main`
# ran 331 the moment #119 landed, and `test-summary.sh` printed its drift note on every run from
# the first. A note that has never been silent is a note nobody reads, so when it names a
# module, refresh this file and this line together.
cli 60
config 64
registry 109
wm 114
